course: history
lesson: 6
title: Ancient Egypt: pyramids, mummies and hieroglyphs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Egyptians believed life went on after death, and that the body was still needed there. That one belief explains the pyramids, the mummies and much of what we know about them.
explain: The Great Pyramid was built about 4,500 years ago for the pharaoh Khufu, not by slaves or aliens but by paid workers and farmers taking turns, who lived in a town nearby.
explain: Think of moving a fridge across a sandy beach: you would put it on a sledge and wet the sand so it slides. Egyptian workers did just that with blocks of stone, then dragged them up ramps.
explain: Bodies were dried with a special salt and wrapped in linen to make mummies, nothing like the walking mummies of films. Egyptians also wrote with hieroglyphs, which nobody could read for over a thousand years until the Rosetta Stone gave the key.
fact: The Great Pyramid of Giza was built about 4,500 years ago for the pharaoh Khufu and was the tallest building in the world for nearly 4,000 years.
fact: The pyramids were built by paid workers and farmers taking turns, who lived in a nearby town; not by slaves, and certainly not by aliens.
fact: Workers cut stone blocks with copper tools, dragged them on sledges over sand wetted to make it slippery, and moved them up ramps.
fact: The Great Sphinx is a huge statue with a lion's body and a pharaoh's head, carved from the rock next to the pyramids; its nose was damaged long before Napoleon's soldiers came.
fact: Egyptians believed the body was needed in the afterlife, so they dried it with a special salt, wrapped it in linen and placed it in a decorated coffin.
fact: Egyptians also mummified millions of animals, such as cats, birds and crocodiles, often as gifts to the gods.
fact: Egyptians believed that after death your heart was weighed against a feather, and a heart not heavy with wrongdoing could pass to a happy afterlife.
fact: Later pharaohs were buried in hidden rock-cut tombs in the Valley of the Kings, because robbers could easily find the pyramids.
fact: Hieroglyphs were Egyptian writing with hundreds of signs; some stood for sounds, like letters, and some for whole words.
fact: Egyptians made a kind of paper from the papyrus reed that grew by the Nile, and our word paper comes from papyrus.
fact: The Rosetta Stone, found in 1799, has the same message in hieroglyphs, another Egyptian script and Greek; scholars could already read the Greek, so they could compare it with the hieroglyphs.
fact: In 1822 Jean-François Champollion used the Rosetta Stone to read hieroglyphs again.
fact: Egyptian doctors set broken bones, stitched wounds and wrote medical books; some cures worked and others were spells.
exercise: For which pharaoh was the Great Pyramid built? | Khufu | Tutankhamun | Cleopatra | Ramesses II
exercise: About how long ago was the Great Pyramid built? | About 4,500 years ago | About 450 years ago | About 45,000 years ago | About 2,000 years ago
exercise: Who built the pyramids? | Paid workers and farmers taking turns | Slaves in chains brought from Rome | Aliens who came down in flying machines | Giants who lived by the Nile
exercise: Why did workers wet the sand in front of a sledge? | To make it slippery so the sledge slid easily | To cool the stones down in the heat | To make mud bricks on the spot for the workers' houses | To wash the stone blocks clean
exercise: What tools did workers use to cut stone? | Copper tools | Steel chainsaws | Bright laser beams | Iron drills with motors
exercise: What does the Great Sphinx look like? | A lion's body with a pharaoh's head | An eagle with a man's body and arms | A crocodile with a cat's head | A horse with wings
exercise: Why did Egyptians make mummies? | They believed the body was needed in the afterlife | To scare away robbers from the tombs | To keep bodies as statues for parks | Because the law said all dead bodies must be painted gold
exercise: What were mummies wrapped in? | Linen bandages | Plastic cling film | Thin gold sheets | Leaves of papyrus only
exercise: Why were animals such as cats mummified? | Often as gifts to the gods | To be eaten later in winter | To use as toys | To sell to the Romans as pets
exercise: What was a heart weighed against after death? | A feather | A golden coin | A stone block | A loaf of bread
exercise: Why were later pharaohs buried in the Valley of the Kings? | Robbers could easily find the pyramids | The pyramids had all fallen down by then | The valley was nearer the sea | Pyramids were too cold
exercise: What were hieroglyphs? | Egyptian writing with hundreds of signs | Egyptian coins | Egyptian boats | Egyptian gods with animal heads and human bodies
exercise: What did Egyptians make paper-like sheets from? | The papyrus reed | Tree bark from Europe | Silk from China | Clay from rivers
exercise: Which three scripts are on the Rosetta Stone? | Hieroglyphs, another Egyptian script and Greek | Latin, Chinese and English | Cuneiform, runes and Hebrew | Only hieroglyphs, three times
exercise: Who first read hieroglyphs again in 1822? | Jean-François Champollion | Napoleon Bonaparte | Howard Carter | Julius Caesar
exercise: What could Egyptian doctors do? | Set broken bones and stitch wounds | Do X-rays and give vaccines | Nothing at all, they only used spells and charms | Transplant hearts
test: a youtube video says aliens built the pyramids bc humans couldn't. what's the truth? | Egyptian workers did it with sledges, ramps and teamwork | Aliens did help with the heavy top stones, the rest was people | Nobody built them, they are hills | Romans built them later
test: In The Mummy film mummies chase people. What are real mummies? | Carefully preserved bodies that cannot move | Sleeping people who wake up at night and walk | Statues made of bandages | Robots from ancient times
test: Uncle says Napoleon's soldiers shot the Sphinx's nose off. Right? | No, the nose was damaged long before | Yes, with a big cannon in 1799 | Yes, and they took the nose back to Paris | No, the Sphinx never had a nose
test: Why would a pharaoh want his tomb hidden instead of a huge pyramid? | So robbers could not find and empty it | Pyramids were too small for him | Hidden tombs were warmer | He wanted to save his money for wars against his enemies
test: If the Rosetta Stone had only hieroglyphs on it, what would be different? | It would not have helped to read them | It would have been easier to read | Champollion would have read it faster | Nothing would change at all
test: A sign is shaped like a bird but stands for a sound. Is that possible in hieroglyphs? | Yes, many signs stood for sounds like letters | No, every sign was just a picture of a thing | No, hieroglyphs had no birds | Yes, but only on Tuesdays
test: Your heart, in Egyptian belief, is lighter than the feather. What happens? | You may pass to a happy afterlife | You must come back as a cat | You become a pharaoh | Nothing, the scales are thrown away
test: Why did the Great Pyramid hold the record for tallest building for so long? | Nobody built anything taller for nearly 4,000 years | It kept growing taller each year as sand piled up on top | People forgot how to build at all | It was the only building in the world
test: Where does our word paper come from? | From papyrus, the Egyptian reed | From the Latin for tree | From the name of a Chinese city | From the sound of tearing
test: The workers lived in a town near the pyramids, with bakeries. What does this suggest? | They were organised, fed workers, not slaves in chains | They were rich tourists there on holiday | They lived inside the pyramids | They were robbers waiting there to break in after the funeral
test: Some Egyptian cures worked and some were spells. Which one probably worked? | Setting a broken arm straight | Saying a magic word at a snake | Wearing a lucky beetle | Painting an eye on the door
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the labour force employed in the construction of the pyramids of Giza? | Remunerated workers and rotating agricultural labourers | Enslaved captives from Rome | Beings of extraterrestrial origin | Prisoners condemned for life
exam: Identify the artefact, discovered in 1799, whereby the decipherment of hieroglyphs was rendered possible. | The Rosetta Stone | The Code of Hammurabi | The Terracotta Army | The Diamond Sutra of China
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which later pharaohs were interred in concealed tombs within the Valley of the Kings. | Pyramids were readily located by tomb robbers | Pyramids had been prohibited by Rome | The valley was situated upon the sea | The pyramids were insufficiently tall
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the purpose of mummification. | The body was believed to be required in the afterlife | It served as a deterrent to robbers | It was a means of preparing food for the gods | It was prescribed solely for animals
